# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is not.
# NEVER put a real value in this file.
#
# The template itself needs NO environment variables — a fresh clone builds and
# deploys with this file untouched. This file exists so that when a client site
# adds an integration (a CRM, a mail provider, an analytics key), the variable
# gets documented HERE, with:
#   - what it is and where to get it
#   - which route/lib reads it
#   - what happens at runtime when it's absent
#
# ⚠️ BUILD-ENV Law (AGENTS.md): the build must succeed with no env vars set.
# Read secrets LAZILY, inside the request handler — never at module scope — so
# CI and preview builds run env-naked. If a secret is missing at RUNTIME, fail
# soft and log; never take the build down.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- First-party analytics relay (src/app/api/t/route.ts + AnalyticsBeacon) --
# GA4 Measurement Protocol credentials: GA4 Admin → Data streams → your stream
# → Measurement Protocol API secrets. Absent: the beacon posts, the relay
# answers 204 and forwards nothing — the site works normally with no analytics.
# GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=
# GA4_API_SECRET=

# --- Meta pixel (src/app/components/MetaPixel.tsx) ---------------------------
# Absent: the component renders nothing. Public by nature (NEXT_PUBLIC_).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=

# EXAMPLE_API_TOKEN=

# --- Studio (/admin publishing dashboard) ------------------------------------
# Optional. /admin needs the values ABOVE the LEGACY divider; with any of them
# missing it shows a "not configured" page and the rest of the site is
# unaffected. Read lazily at request time (src/lib/admin/env.ts) — the build
# needs none of them.
#
# Identity project: ONE Supabase project shared by every site you run, holding
# who may publish to which site. PUBLIC by design — RLS is the boundary.
# Dashboard → Project Settings → API.
NEXT_PUBLIC_IDENTITY_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_IDENTITY_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# This site's stable slug in the identity DB (site_users.site_id). Lowercase,
# hyphens only, never the repo name — a repo rename must not orphan users.
ADMIN_SITE_ID=
# The repository Studio publishes to, as owner/repo. REWRITE THIS ON TRANSFER —
# the owner changes when the repo moves to someone else.
GITHUB_REPO=owner/repo
# SERVER-ONLY. This site's own credential for the Realiiz service. The repo's
# owner installs the Realiiz Studio app once
# (github.com/apps/realiiz-studio/installations/new, pick this repo) and the site
# is issued a credential automatically. With these two the deployment holds NO
# master key: it asks Realiiz for a token scoped to THIS ONE repository, good for
# an hour, and for invites to be sent. A lost secret is replaced by issuing a new
# one. This pair is what /admin should run on; everything under LEGACY below is
# what it replaces.
REALIIZ_BROKER_URL=https://broker.realiiz.com
REALIIZ_SITE_SECRET=
# SERVER-ONLY, OPTIONAL. A Vercel Deploy Hook for the production branch (Vercel →
# Project → Settings → Git → Deploy Hooks → Create, branch "main"). Very rarely
# Vercel misses the push when a Studio update merges and the site never rebuilds;
# with this set, the update tracker offers owners a "Nudge the build" button that
# asks Vercel again. Without it the tracker says to contact the web team. Set it —
# it's the difference between a client fixing this themselves and a support call.
VERCEL_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL=
# Supabase → Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs must list, per host:
#   {host}/admin/auth/callback   (sign-in links)   and   {host}/admin/auth/accept   (invitations)
# Exact matches only — no query strings. A mismatch silently lands on the Site URL.

# ─── LEGACY — never set on a new site, never hand to anyone ───────────────────
# Older ways for Studio to reach the repo and the identity project, kept only
# because sites deployed before the Realiiz service still run on them. Every one
# is a SHARED key: the App private key acts on EVERY repository the Realiiz
# Studio app is installed on, and the service-role key bypasses RLS on the
# identity project that every site shares — so one deployment's environment
# leaking compromises all of them.
#
# Everything above this divider is safe to set. Nothing below it is. Set
# REALIIZ_BROKER_URL and REALIIZ_SITE_SECRET instead and leave these empty.
#
# The GitHub App's own key — the SAME on every site; the .pem GitHub generated,
# pasted whole (newlines and all; a single line with literal \n also works).
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# A personal access token for THIS repo — Contents RW, Pull requests RW,
# Commit statuses R, Deployments R.
GITHUB_CONTENT_TOKEN=
# The identity project's service-role key (Dashboard → Project Settings → API →
# service_role). Only the Team tab ever used it — inviting people, changing
# roles, resending invites. The broker now does that work itself (broker-team.ts
# in @realiizlabs/admin), where the key stays on Realiiz's side, so a site on the
# broker never needs this one.
IDENTITY_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# TEMPORARY — the client-review kit. Where the preview's feedback notes and
# "Approve this version" go: an inbound-webhook URL whose workflow emails you.
# Absent, the route logs the note and answers { delivered: false }, and the
# widget falls back to a pre-filled email to PREVIEW_FEEDBACK_EMAIL. Remove with
# the review kit at launch.
# FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
